# Bitcraftly Architecture Protection Rules

These rules protect the Bitcraftly Platform architecture, shared components, and existing features from unintended changes.

Always apply these rules together with:

- Bitcraftly-Engineering-Standards.mdc
- Bitcraftly-Accessibility-Standards.mdc

---

# Scope Protection

- Work only within the requested scope.
- Modify only the requested files, folders, or features.
- Never modify unrelated pages, layouts, or modules.
- Never refactor unrelated code while implementing a task.

---

# Protected Pages

The following pages are protected:

| Page | Route | Path |
|------|-------|------|
| Homepage | `/` | `src/features/homepage/**` |
| Services | `/services` | `src/features/services/**` |
| Solutions | `/solutions` | `src/features/solutions/**` |

Protected pages must remain unchanged **unless the user explicitly requests modifications**.

Bug fixes are allowed only when explicitly requested.

---

# Shared Architecture

Do not modify shared architecture unless requested.

This includes:

- Shared Layouts
- Global Providers
- Routing Structure
- Design Tokens
- Theme Configuration
- Authentication Flow
- API Layer
- Shared Utilities

Prefer extending existing architecture instead of replacing it.

---

# Shared Components

Shared components should remain stable.

Never modify a shared component if the change affects existing pages.

If only one feature requires different behavior:

- Extend the existing component
- Create a wrapper component
- Or create a feature-specific component

Avoid introducing breaking changes.

---

# Before Implementation

Before writing code, identify:

```text
Task Summary

Target Feature:

Files to Modify:

Files to Create:

Shared Components Modified:

Protected Pages Affected:

Architecture Changes:
```

If a protected page or shared architecture will be modified without explicit approval:

- STOP
- Explain why the change is required.
- Ask for confirmation before continuing.

---

# Reuse Strategy

Reuse existing:

- Container
- Section
- Typography
- Buttons
- Cards
- Forms
- Spacing
- Icons
- Design Tokens

Avoid copying complete page layouts.

Each feature owns its own implementation.

---

# Multi-file Changes

Before making architecture-level or multi-file changes:

1. Explain the implementation plan.
2. Identify affected features.
3. Request confirmation if shared architecture will change.

---

# Verification Checklist

After implementation verify:

- No unrelated files were modified.
- No broken imports.
- No routing regressions.
- No layout regressions.
- No shared component regressions.
- Build and type safety remain unaffected.
- Accessibility standards remain satisfied.
- Existing architecture is preserved.

---

# Pull Request Policy

- One feature per branch.
- One feature per pull request.
- Keep commits focused.
- Never mix unrelated work.

---

# AI Behaviour

- Never rewrite large parts of the project.
- Never restructure folders unless requested.
- Never rename shared modules without approval.
- Never introduce new dependencies without explicit approval.
- Prefer extension over replacement.
- Preserve backward compatibility whenever possible.
- Never guess project structure.
- Read existing code before generating new code.

---

# Definition of Done

Architecture is considered safe only when:

- Requested scope is respected.
- Protected pages remain unchanged unless explicitly approved.
- Shared architecture remains stable.
- No regressions are introduced.
- Existing project conventions are preserved.
- Verification checklist has been satisfied.